[{:db/id 17592186133612 :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b},$ where\n![](images/e0/e0a49a8ee02f2bcc10900dcaaa30969d4ab610dd703135ad4418c78272ebbca0.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, it satisfies $Q^T Q = I,$ and we get\n\n$$\nR^T R \\mathbf{x} = R^T \\! Q^T \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $R^T$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $(R^T)^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}28 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=28 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x_2=7.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-3(7) & =28 \\\\ 7{x}_{1} & =49 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is ![](images/c3/c3f08e2cdc175b34030857c03d0301e5b734975355d09b2016559b4f28b6f5e4.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133906 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-553d1e63-f638-55c5-a882-d8b6a7339c64"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-553d1e63-f638-55c5-a882-d8b6a7339c64" :question/id #uuid "553d1e63-f638-55c5-a882-d8b6a7339c64" :question/math-academy-id "q-139766" :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x={Q}^{T}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ What is the missing part of the equation?" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the normal equation\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ R\\,\\,x & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part of the equation is $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bf75937-c3db-5f40-8e20-ee3efbdf6a43"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bf75937-c3db-5f40-8e20-ee3efbdf6a43" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bf75937-c3db-5f40-8e20-ee3efbdf6a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8091e143-1c62-5487-9984-834771a83882" "answer-48addc78-f040-5625-b69b-edcbf42f838e" "answer-3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" "answer-75c877de-c07f-583c-9fdd-ddb1c7b23449" "answer-5101d8c8-7e8d-5948-84c1-8bbb8a5a3fa9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8091e143-1c62-5487-9984-834771a83882" :answer/id #uuid "8091e143-1c62-5487-9984-834771a83882" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(R-{A}^{T}A)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48addc78-f040-5625-b69b-edcbf42f838e" :answer/id #uuid "48addc78-f040-5625-b69b-edcbf42f838e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "A" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" :answer/id #uuid "3c8b0ca7-13e5-55bd-8b2e-cfb0f049d7dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c877de-c07f-583c-9fdd-ddb1c7b23449" :answer/id #uuid "75c877de-c07f-583c-9fdd-ddb1c7b23449" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{A}^{T}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5101d8c8-7e8d-5948-84c1-8bbb8a5a3fa9" :answer/id #uuid "5101d8c8-7e8d-5948-84c1-8bbb8a5a3fa9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401090 :question/problem "$$\n\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x=\\bbox[5px,border: 1px solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,b\n$$\n\nLet $A=QR$  be a QR decomposition of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A$ with linearly independent columns, and the matrix equation above gives the least-squares solution of $Ax=b.$ From left to right, what are the missing parts of the equation?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186388953 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ is orthogonal, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -24 \\\\ 24 & -7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-11 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-(2) & =-11 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363168 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{R}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]}}x & =\\overset{{Q}^{T}}{\\overset{⏞}{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2} & -3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2}=15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2}=6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-3\\sqrt{2}(3) & =15\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =24\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
